\section{A report is not a runtime layout}

Steele and Sussman's January 1978 \emph{Revised Report on Scheme: A Dialect of Lisp} describes procedure values that close over the lexical environment of their definition, and tail recursion that executes without net growth of the control stack. Its treatment of explicitly fluid variables and escape objects belongs to that dialect; it is not a specification for their names or interfaces in a modern implementation. The \emph{Revised$^{7}$ Report on the Algorithmic Language Scheme}, edited by Alex Shinn, John Cowan, and Arthur A. Gleckler, turns the control intuition into a requirement to support an unbounded number of active tail calls. The July 2013 report and the February 2021 text retain both the tail-context rules and the location model.

The location model says that a variable occurrence names a location and that reading it obtains the value most recently stored there. It does not require a pointer or a heap allocation for every binding. A register can represent an unaliased location; two closures that share an assigned binding, however, must observe the same updates. Conversely, distinct live instances of a parameter binding must not be merged simply because they arise from the same source parameter. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-165} shows when a compiler has to make that distinction explicit in its environment layout. In \emph{A Semantic Comparison of Lisp and Scheme}, Steven S.\ Muchnick and Uwe F.\ Pleban study the compositional semantics of the 1978 Scheme in contrast to that of dialects with FEXPRs, which receive unevaluated arguments. A modern optimizer should likewise establish which forms are applications before reasoning about their evaluation: familiar Lisp notation is not evidence that two dialects evaluate the same subexpressions.

\section{Classifying specification clauses}

For each requirement, record the source construct, any side conditions, and what an observer can check. A procedure's lexical scope and the branch selected by a false conditional are fixed behavior. An unspecified evaluation order is a choice among allowed behaviors, not a license to omit an operand. An error case carries a different obligation: R7RS distinguishes ``it is an error'' from requirements to signal an error. Mutating a literal pair is an error; tests should not demand a particular result or exception for such a program. Likewise, the absence of a guarantee about object identity in a report provision is not an opportunity for a portability test to infer identity from a particular allocator.

The distinction matters for primitive recognition. In \emph{The Scheme 311 Compiler}, William Clinger chose right-to-left operand evaluation and adjusted his proof's semantics to make initial primitive bindings directly denote primitives while still allowing local shadowing. These are separate proof choices. Committing to an allowed order can implement a report that leaves it open; changing what an existing binding denotes cannot be justified by that same permission. A source optimizer should recognize a primitive by resolved binding and the validity of the value assumption, not by the spelling of the operator.

\section{Worked contract: assign, then apply}

In a program importing \texttt{(scheme base)}, both operands of the outer call below have effects. The test uses fresh mutable data rather than a quoted list:

\begin{verbatim}
(let ((p (cons 0 0)))
  (let ((observed
         ((lambda (a b) (list a b))
          (begin (set-car! p 1) (car p))
          (begin (set-car! p 2) (car p)))))
    (list observed (car p))))
\end{verbatim}

The operands must behave consistently with some sequential order. The valid results are \verb|((1 2) 2)| and \verb|((1 2) 1)|. The first element is fixed: position in the argument list is independent of evaluation order. Reading the final state before the application has finished or interleaving the two operand evaluations so that both reads see the same value fails the contract. A separate ordered control uses two successive expressions in a \texttt{begin}; for that variant a single order is required. Pairing these tests catches both a too-strict conformance harness and a compiler that reorders effects outside the permitted regions.

\Cref{fig:visual-161} shows the difference between order of
