\section{The library boundary}

R7RS-small's \texttt{define-library}, \texttt{import}, and \texttt{export} put a public name and a local binding on different sides of an interface. Resolve import modifiers such as \texttt{rename} before treating uses as references to a variable or transformer. A renamed transformer must still refer to the bindings of its definition environment. Feature selection with \texttt{cond-expand} is not a runtime test on a variable, so the harness should record the feature set used to expand the library. A useful test imports a library under both a prefix and a rename, then checks that an exported macro continues to use the library's private helper even when the importing program binds a homonym. Together, the tests check the report at three boundaries: evaluation, storage, and syntax. Chapter~\ref{ch:compiler-testing} develops the broader method of using semantic properties instead of a single reference output.
