\section{Testing a Tail Call Through an Indirect Operator}

A compiler that turns self-recursion into a jump may still fail
Scheme's proper tail-call requirement. For a test that exercises
another path, build two procedures that tail-call each other
through a parameter. Each receives a countdown and a procedure
argument naming its successor; the two procedures pass their own
names back as the next successor. One returns $10$ at zero and
the other returns $20$. The other clause uses the application's
result directly in the tail position of an \texttt{if}. Use
exact nonnegative integers and standard lexical bindings. The
portable observation is which number the call returns; in a
test build, collect a separate high-water mark of pending Scheme
return obligations, not of total allocated bytes.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input countdown $n\ge0$, entry procedure $a$
  \Output result, plus optional control high-water mark
  \Function{A}{$k,next$}
    \If{$k=0$}
      \State \Return $10$
    \EndIf
    \State \Return $\Call{Indirect}{next,k-1,a}$
    \Comment{The call inherits the surrounding continuation}
  \EndFunction
  \Function{B}{$k,next$}
    \If{$k=0$}
      \State \Return $20$
    \EndIf
    \State \Return $\Call{Indirect}{next,k-1,b}$
    \Comment{Different lexical procedure identity}
  \EndFunction
  \State \Return $\Call{A}{n,b}$
\end{sudocode}

For $n=3$, the calls are $A(3,b)$, $B(2,a)$, $A(1,b)$,
and $B(0,a)$. The result is $20$; starting at $n=4$ returns
$10$. Repeat with larger counts of each parity. Each hot call
goes through the procedure parameter, so record whether the
implementation specialized it into a known call. To test
generic tail application in a particular compiler, prevent
that specialization in the instrumented build or provide
the initial procedures through a separately compiled interface.

After each transfer the countdown decreases by one and the
current procedure changes; there is no additional work to
complete inside the previous procedure. Operand computations
may themselves need temporary state, but the tail application
must not leave a pending return to the previous step. A finite
stress test cannot prove proper tail recursion, and a bounded
process heap is not a portable requirement. The control
measurement helps distinguish a missed tail transfer from a
separate source of allocation or resource exhaustion.
